n: 3.7.3.3
name: refusal-against-silent-recovery
kind: finding
is: The owner's direction of 2026-09-09, 'form native does not refuse, it heals and integrates or releases', and the lesson that failure was recovered silently into a value, the study's fourth-ranked cause, are compatible only if a fault is a loud, named signal carrying its repair, which the running flow answers with another choice. A fault must never turn into a number.
status: inferred
conf: mid
src: 2.10.45#8 2.10.45#26-29 2.10.1#RC4
from: 3.7.2.5 3.7.2.6 1.1
limit: The study did not verify that the new grammar's fail, stop and choice can meet this obligation. Its line 287 calls silent recovery 'the most damaging failure class' without a measure; its own ranking at lines 87 and 132 puts it fourth of twelve.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

n: 3.7.4
name: worked
kind: hub
is: Seven things the records show working in the old body: node ledgers with atomic writes, control words with journaled undo, one definition per operation, an emitter in the language beside a 1 ms sampler, content-keyed stores, a second reader that exposed faults, in-process cells with event waits. Most figures are its own records on one machine.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

n: 3.7.4.1
name: node-ledgers-and-atomic-write
kind: observation
is: The old body measured 3,000 ledger rows held as nodes: built in 25 to 34 ms, written in 2 ms and read back in 3 ms with the same ids, against 130 ms to write and 55 ms to read the same rows as text. write_form_binary writes beside the file and renames over it, so a reader meets a whole file or the last whole one.
status: observed
src: 2.10.4#47-48 2.10.4#52-54 2.10.28#13
limit: The figures are the document's record of 2026-10-12; the study did not re-run the measurement.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

n: 3.7.4.2
name: control-vocabulary-with-undo
kind: observation
is: The old body's floor document lists its control words (choice {} or {}, fail, cut, stop, try {} catch {}, timeout N {}, store()/undo()) and says a failed option's writes are undone by a journal, printed lines included, and its finding reaches the next option. A rule of 2026-11-12 adds that a retry must differ and carry what the last attempt saw.
status: observed
src: 2.1.6#1198-1199 2.10.46#16
limit: Read in the floor document; the study ran no program that uses these words.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

n: 3.7.4.3
name: one-definition-per-operation
kind: observation
is: The old body's repair for heat-dependent meaning: every strict op is one C function (fk_op_*) called by walker and compiled page alike, a call has one meaning, all machine code is born through one page door, and anf-lower.bml binds every argument, in order, before its call. substring and str_find are specified as total, clamping and byte-indexed.
status: observed
src: 2.1.6#1296-1310 2.1.6#1004 2.10.3#15-26 2.10.2#16-30
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

n: 3.7.4.4
name: emitter-in-the-language
kind: observation
is: jit-page.bml, written in the old body's own language, reads a definition's nodes in-process, writes AArch64 words and binds them; a 3-million-step tail loop took 156 ms walked and 7 ms as a page, plus 7 ms for the first offer. Its tier-up counts time as well as calls, the C loop lane's decline codes name the missing arm, and a 1 ms sampler credits each sample to a phase or recipe.
status: observed
src: 2.1.6#1276-1282 2.1.6#1337-1344 2.1.6#1222-1234 2.10.37#13-21 2.1.7#12602 2.1.7#14476-14479
limit: The figures are the floor document's records on one machine. The decline code -(1000 + tag) is set in the C lane's fk_f64_* functions of the seed, and the arms the cited note added went in through fk_f64_ccall; no decline code of jit-page.bml was read. The study did not run the emitter and did not read jit-page.bml itself.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/lessons

